WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.688
Custom licenses: read for the conditions.

2
00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:06.979
The other large group is custom licenses written by the publisher.

3
00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:11.120
They usually allow commercial use, and the risk is in the conditions.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.368
Five kinds of clause come up again and again, and they are what you scan for.

5
00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.483
User or revenue thresholds.

6
00:00:17.763 --> 00:00:27.768
The Llama 4 Community License requires a separate license from Meta if your products had more than 700 million monthly active users on the release date.

7
00:00:28.048 --> 00:00:31.419
Newer custom licenses use revenue: the Qwen3.

8
00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:35.048
8-Max License (for Qwen’s largest Qwen3.

9
00:00:35.328 --> 00:00:51.605
8 model) requires products above 100 million monthly users or 20 million dollars in monthly revenue to display the model’s name, and requires a separate license for model-as-a-service businesses above 50 million dollars of revenue over 12 months.

10
00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.720
Model-as-a-service restrictions.

11
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.141
Several licenses treat hosting the model for others differently from building products with it.

12
00:00:59.421 --> 00:01:09.171
Mistral Large 4’s Ecosystem Open-Weight License is a modified MIT license under which model-as-a-service businesses need a commercial license.

13
00:01:09.451 --> 00:01:11.029
Moonshot’s Kimi K3.

14
00:01:11.309 --> 00:01:22.104
1-Pro license and MiniMax’s Community License have comparable clauses, and MiniMax’s base grant covers non-commercial use only, with conditions for commercial use.

15
00:01:22.384 --> 00:01:23.899
Naming and attribution.

16
00:01:24.179 --> 00:01:32.264
Llama 4 requires you to display “Built with Llama,” and a derivative model you distribute must have a name that begins with “Llama.

17
00:01:32.544 --> 00:01:32.842
”.

18
00:01:33.122 --> 00:01:34.210
Use policies.

19
00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:52.260
Llama 4 incorporates an acceptable use policy; the Gemma Terms of Use (which cover Gemma releases before Gemma 4) incorporate a Prohibited Use Policy, require you to pass the restrictions on to anyone you distribute to, and let Google restrict use remotely or otherwise.

20
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.532
Use policies can change after you adopt the model, so note the version you agreed to.

21
00:01:57.812 --> 00:01:59.028
Regional limits.

22
00:01:59.308 --> 00:02:12.834
The Llama 4 acceptable use policy withholds rights to the multimodal Llama 4 models from individuals and companies whose principal place of business is in the EU (end users of products built on them are exempt).

23
00:02:13.114 --> 00:02:15.908
Hugging Face even has a gating setting for this.

24
00:02:16.188 --> 00:02:19.004
Licenses also change between generations.

25
00:02:19.284 --> 00:02:22.484
Gemma moved from the Gemma Terms of Use to Apache 2.

26
00:02:22.764 --> 00:02:25.964
0 with Gemma 4, and NVIDIA’s Nemotron 3.

27
00:02:26.244 --> 00:02:28.996
5 models use OpenMDW 1.

28
00:02:29.276 --> 00:02:32.476
1, a permissive license written for models and data.

29
00:02:32.756 --> 00:02:36.404
Always read the license for the exact model and version you ship.

30
00:02:36.684 --> 00:02:47.948
On the Hugging Face Hub, a repo whose card says license: other keeps the actual terms in a LICENSE file and names the license in license_name; that file is the one to read.
